A computer player taking control of a turn (Commander Duels v0.6.1, rule 723), in every format.

Reason: XMage refuses every computer player the control of another player's turn ("is AI and can't
take control"), so a bot's Mindslaver, Word of Command or Opposition Agent loses its control. With
this patch the computer player makes the controlled player's decisions (723.5) through an AI
stand-in: ComputerPlayerControllableProxy.prepareControllableProxy makes one for each decision, a
copy of the controlled player that decides with the controller's AI (its skill, depth and think
time) and reads and changes the controlled player's own hand, library, graveyard, mana pool, life
and land count. A human player whose turn a computer player controls hands every decision to it
(HumanPlayer, computerDeciding) and is asked nothing; a computer player under a computer player's
control already handed its decisions to its controller's stand-in, which was the controller itself.
A computer player that makes no stand-in is still refused (Player.canControlOtherPlayers). The
stand-in plays as XMage's AI plays for the player it stands in for, not against them. Commander
Duels' fair bot (engine/src/main/java/com/commanderduels/FairBot.java) makes its stand-ins as fair
bots.
Upstream: none yet; XMage's own TODOs ("add support computer over computer", "computer over human")
ask for it.
Remove when: XMage upstream lets computer players control turns.

